      "text": "\n\nBelow is a practical, step-by-step debugging plan for a slow reporting query in PostgreSQL after a schema migration.\n\n## 1. Reproduce and capture the baseline\n\n- Run the exact query in the same environment (same data volume, same session settings).\n- Capture:\n  - `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, FORMAT TEXT)` output.\n  - `pg_stat_statements` entry for the query (if enabled) to see average/total time and calls.\n  - `pg_stat_user_tables` for the involved tables (seq scans, index scans, n_tup_ins/upd/del).\n- Note the query plan before any changes.\n\n## 2. EXPLAIN ANALYZE: what to look for\n\nRun:\n\n```sql\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING, FORMAT TEXT)\nSELECT ...\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nJOIN invoices i ON i.order_id = o.id\nLEFT JOIN refunds r ON r.invoice_id = i.id\nWHERE o.tenant_id = $1\n  AND o.created_at >= $2\n  AND o.created_at < $3\nGROUP BY c.segment;\n```\n\nFocus on:\n\n- **Actual rows vs estimated rows**: Large discrepancies indicate stale or missing statistics.\n- **Node types**:\n  - `Seq Scan` on large tables where an index should be used.\n  - `Nested Loop` with high `loops` and high `actual time` per loop.\n  - `Hash Join` / `Merge Join` with high memory usage or spills to disk.\n- **Buffers**:\n  - `shared hit` vs `shared read`: high `shared read` means data is not cached; consider caching or reducing I/O.\n  - `temp read` / `temp write`: indicates hash/merge join or sort spilling to disk.\n- **Planning time vs execution time**: If planning time is high, the planner is struggling (many tables, complex joins, or bad statistics).\n\n## 3. Check indexes\n\n### 3.1 Identify missing or unused indexes\n\n- Look at the plan: if a `Seq Scan` appears on `orders` filtered by `tenant_id` and `created_at`, you likely need a composite index.\n- Check existing indexes:\n\n```sql\nSELECT indexname, indexdef\nFROM pg_indexes\nWHERE tablename IN ('orders",
      "text_preview": "\n\nBelow is a practical, step-by-step debugging plan for a slow reporting query in PostgreSQL after a schema migration.\n\n## 1. Reproduce and capture the baseline\n\n- Run the exact query in the same environment (same data volume, same session settings).\n- Capture:\n  - `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, FORMAT TEXT)` output.\n  - ",
